\title{Deterministic Causal Loops, Fixed-Point Consistency,\\and Acyclic Branching Histories}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 22, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We present a minimal discrete mathematics of causal loops and branching timelines. A deterministic causal law is a map $f:X\to X$, and a closed orbit of positive period $p$ is a state $x$ satisfying $f^p(x)=x$. We distinguish periodic closure from pointwise Novikov consistency, which requires every visited state $f^k(x)$, for $0\leq k<p$, to be fixed by $f$. On any nonempty deterministic closed orbit, pointwise consistency is equivalent to the presence of a fixed point. The mechanism is a collapse principle: one fixed point on a deterministic closed orbit forces the entire orbit to equal that point. For idempotent laws satisfying $f^2=f$, every positive closed orbit collapses automatically.

Boolean negation provides a minimal grandfather-intervention model. It has no fixed point; odd-period closure is impossible; and every positive even period closes while remaining inconsistent. We then replace history-overwriting by a finite-prefix branching model. An intervention appends an event to a finite history, producing a strict descendant. Strict descent is transitive and irreflexive, while distinct interventions produce incomparable sibling branches. The results distinguish recurrence, equilibrium, contradiction, and branch creation without making claims about the physical existence of time travel.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Time-travel paradoxes are often expressed as narratives in which an event causes an intervention that prevents the event itself. Their logical core can be studied with a state space, a rule for causal succession, and a precise meaning of consistency. A process may return to its starting state without being stable at any intermediate point. This is the familiar dynamical distinction between a periodic orbit and an equilibrium, recast as the distinction between closure and local self-consistency.

We examine two models. The first describes deterministic causal succession by a function and treats a closed history as a periodic point. We impose a strong pointwise Novikov condition: every state encountered before closure must be fixed under the causal law. The second model is branching. A timeline is a finite sequence, an intervention appends a new event, and ancestry is prefix inclusion. The first model identifies exactly when a closed loop is consistent; the second prevents overwrite paradoxes through acyclic extension.

These are logical models, not a theory of relativistic spacetime. A closed timelike curve is geometric, whereas consistency is relative to an evolution law for matter or information. That boundary is maintained throughout.

\section{Deterministic causal dynamics}
Let $X$ be a set of event-states. A deterministic causal law is a function $f:X\to X$. Write $f^0$ for the identity and $f^{n+1}=f\circ f^n$. Thus
\[
f^{m+n}(x)=f^m\bigl(f^n(x)\bigr).
\]

\begin{definition}[Closed orbit]
For $p\in\mathbb N$ and $x\in X$, the orbit from $x$ is closed after $p$ steps if $f^p(x)=x$. The substantive case is $p>0$, since $f^0(x)=x$ automatically.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Pointwise Novikov consistency]
The length-$p$ orbit from $x$ is pointwise self-consistent if
\[
f\bigl(f^k(x)\bigr)=f^k(x)
\]
for every integer $k$ with $0\leq k<p$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Fixed point on a loop]
The length-$p$ orbit from $x$ contains a fixed point if there is an index $k$ with $0\leq k<p$ such that $f(f^k(x))=f^k(x)$.
\end{definition}

Pointwise consistency is universal over the visited states, while fixed-point existence is existential. Determinism and closure make these conditions equivalent.

\begin{lemma}[Consistency supplies a fixed point]
Let $p>0$. If the length-$p$ orbit from $x$ is pointwise Novikov-consistent, then it contains a fixed point.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The index $k=0$ is allowed. Consistency there states $f(f^0(x))=f^0(x)$, or $f(x)=x$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Fixed start gives consistency]
If $f(x)=x$, then $f^n(x)=x$ for all $n\in\mathbb N$. Consequently the length-$p$ orbit from $x$ is pointwise Novikov-consistent for every $p$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Induct on $n$. The zero case is immediate. If $f^n(x)=x$, then $f^{n+1}(x)=f(f^n(x))=f(x)=x$. Every visited state is therefore the same fixed state.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Fixed-event collapse]
Suppose $f^p(x)=x$ and $y=f^k(x)$ is fixed for some $0\leq k<p$. Then $y=x$, $f(x)=x$, and every state on the orbit equals $x$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
From $f(y)=y$, induction gives $f^n(y)=y$ for all $n\geq0$. Since $p=k+(p-k)$,
\[
f^p(x)=f^{p-k}(f^k(x))=f^{p-k}(y)=y.
\]
Closure gives $f^p(x)=x$, hence $x=y$. Thus $f(x)=x$, and the preceding lemma makes every iterate equal to $x$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Novikov fixed-point equivalence]
Let $p>0$ and suppose $f^p(x)=x$. The orbit from $x$ is pointwise Novikov-consistent if and only if it contains a fixed point.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Consistency supplies a fixed point by the first lemma. Conversely, a fixed point on the closed orbit forces the start to be fixed by the collapse lemma, after which the fixed-start lemma gives consistency.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[No mixed deterministic loop]
A positive closed orbit cannot contain both a fixed state and a distinct moving state. If one visited state is fixed, the orbit is constant.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
This is the fixed-event collapse lemma.
\end{proof}

\section{Idempotent laws and automatic collapse}
\begin{definition}[Idempotent causal law]
A causal law is idempotent when $f(f(x))=f(x)$ for every $x\in X$, equivalently $f^2=f$.
\end{definition}

Idempotent maps include projections and canonicalization procedures. Every point in their image is fixed.

\begin{lemma}[Positive iterates are fixed]
If $f$ is idempotent, then for every $x\in X$ and $n>0$,
\[
f^n(x)=f(x),\qquad f(f^n(x))=f^n(x).
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Write $n=m+1$. Starting at $f(x)$, every additional application leaves the state unchanged by idempotence. Induction on $m$ gives the claim.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Idempotent causal-loop collapse]
Let $f$ be idempotent, let $p>0$, and suppose $f^p(x)=x$. Then $f(x)=x$. Consequently the closed orbit is constant and pointwise Novikov-consistent.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The preceding lemma gives $f^p(x)=f(x)$, while closure gives $f^p(x)=x$. Hence $f(x)=x$, and every iterate equals $x$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Three-way characterization]
Under the hypotheses of the idempotent causal-loop collapse theorem, the following are equivalent: the orbit is pointwise Novikov-consistent; the starting state is fixed and the orbit contains a fixed point; and the starting state is fixed.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The collapse theorem gives the fixed-start condition. A consistent positive orbit contains a fixed point. Conversely, a fixed start gives consistency and itself supplies a fixed point at index zero.
\end{proof}

Idempotence is a uniform sufficient condition rather than a necessary condition for any particular orbit. A non-idempotent map may possess fixed points, but an idempotent map rules out every nonconstant positive cycle.

\section{The Boolean grandfather intervention}
Let $B=\{0,1\}$, where $1$ means that an ancestor survives and $0$ means that the ancestor does not survive. Define $g:B\to B$ by $g(a)=1-a$.

\begin{theorem}[No self-consistent grandfather state]
There is no $a\in B$ satisfying $g(a)=a$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
One has $g(0)=1\neq0$ and $g(1)=0\neq1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Parity of iterated intervention]
For every $m\in\mathbb N$ and $a\in B$,
\[
g^{2m}(a)=a,\qquad g^{2m+1}(a)=g(a)=1-a.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Two applications cancel: $g^2(a)=a$. Group an even iterate into pairs; an odd iterate has one unpaired flip.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Odd-period no-go]
For every odd positive integer $p$ and every $a\in B$, $g^p(a)\neq a$. Hence no odd-period grandfather orbit closes.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Write $p=2m+1$. The parity lemma gives $g^p(a)=1-a$, which differs from either Boolean state $a$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Even closure without consistency]
For every positive even integer $p$ and every $a\in B$, $g^p(a)=a$, but the orbit is not pointwise Novikov-consistent. In particular, the two-step orbit closes but is inconsistent.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Write $p=2m$ with $m>0$. Closure follows from the parity lemma. Consistency at index zero would require $g(a)=a$, contradicting the no-fixed-state theorem.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Non-idempotence]
The grandfather law is not idempotent.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
For either state, $g(g(a))=a$ whereas $g(a)=1-a$.
\end{proof}

The example is deterministic and periodic, yet inconsistent. It proves that recurrence does not imply stability and identifies failure of the fixed-point equation as the core contradiction.

\section{Finite branching histories}
Let $E$ be a set of events. A timeline is a finite list $H=[e_1,\ldots,e_n]$ with length $|H|$. Write $H\concat R$ for concatenation.

\begin{definition}[Travel by extension]
For a source timeline $H$ and intervention $a\in E$, define
\[
T(H,a)=H\concat[a].
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Ancestry and strict descent]
A timeline $A$ is an ancestor of $B$ if $A$ is a prefix of $B$, meaning $B=A\concat R$ for some finite list $R$. A timeline $B$ is a strict descendant of $A$ if $A$ is an ancestor of $B$ and $A\neq B$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Source preservation]
For every $H$ and $a$, the source $H$ is an ancestor of $T(H,a)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Use the remainder list $[a]$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Unit length growth]
For every $H$ and $a$, $|T(H,a)|=|H|+1$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Concatenation adds lengths and a singleton has length one.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Branch creation]
For every source $H$ and intervention $a$, $T(H,a)\neq H$, and $T(H,a)$ is a strict descendant of $H$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Equality would imply equal lengths, contrary to unit length growth. Combine this inequality with source preservation.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Transitivity of strict descent]
If $B$ is a strict descendant of $A$ and $C$ is a strict descendant of $B$, then $C$ is a strict descendant of $A$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Prefix transitivity makes $A$ a prefix of $C$. Proper finite prefix extension strictly increases length, so $|A|<|B|<|C|$ and $A\neq C$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Acyclicity]
No timeline is its own strict descendant. Consequently no finite chain of strict branch extensions can return to its starting timeline.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Self-descent requires both equality and inequality with oneself. A returning chain would, by transitivity, imply self-descent. Equivalently, length strictly increases at each step.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Distinct siblings]
If $a,b\in E$ and $a\neq b$, then $T(H,a)\neq T(H,b)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Equality followed by cancellation of the common prefix $H$ gives $[a]=[b]$, hence $a=b$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sibling incomparability]
If $a\neq b$, neither $T(H,a)$ nor $T(H,b)$ is an ancestor of the other.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Both children have length $|H|+1$. A finite list that is a prefix of another list of equal length must equal it, contradicting the distinct-siblings theorem. The argument is symmetric.
\end{proof}

Thus branching travel preserves the source, adds exactly one event, and creates a new child. Distinct interventions are incomparable continuations, not incompatible overwrites of one history.

\section{Algorithms and computational interpretation}
An \emph{orbit audit} receives a finite-state update table, a start $x$, and a positive proposed period $p$. It computes $x,f(x),\ldots,f^p(x)$, tests closure at the endpoint, and tests both existential and universal fixed-point conditions among the first $p$ states. It takes $O(p)$ time. Storing the orbit takes $O(p)$ space, while retaining only status flags takes $O(1)$ auxiliary space. On a closed deterministic orbit, the fixed-point equivalence predicts that the existential and universal tests agree.

For a finite space of $N$ states, an \emph{idempotence audit} checks $f(f(x))=f(x)$ for all $x$. It uses $O(N)$ time and $O(1)$ auxiliary space for a table representation. Success makes every positive closed orbit constant by the idempotent collapse theorem.

A \emph{branch constructor} returns $H\concat[a]$. Immutable arrays require $O(|H|)$ copying, whereas persistent linked structures can share the source prefix and add a node in $O(1)$ time. Explicit prefix comparison takes $O(\min(|A|,|B|))$ time.

\section{Applications and discussion}
In dynamical systems, the Boolean intervention is the smallest nontrivial periodic orbit and illustrates the difference between an equilibrium and a cycle. In distributed systems, a global state can recur while local transitions remain active; recurrence is not quiescence. Idempotent updates are valuable because retries make no further change, exactly the stabilization expressed by the collapse theorem.

Normalization procedures in data processing are often idempotent: once a record is canonical, another normalization leaves it alone. Version control and event-sourced systems instead resemble the branching model. New records extend immutable histories, ancestry is prefix-like, and distinct updates from a shared parent create siblings.

The pointwise consistency condition used here is strong. A relational model could require only that local constraints around a cycle be jointly satisfiable, allowing states to change consistently. The finite-list model also excludes merge operations, erasure, concurrency, branch weights, and conservation laws. Its acyclicity comes specifically from append-only extension.

\section{Future work and geometric boundary}
Nondeterministic causality can be represented by a relation $R\subseteq X\times X$. In that setting one fixed state need not absorb every continuation, so the hypotheses for a fixed-point characterization become substantive. Constraint-based models can assign local relations to the edges of a directed cycle and ask for a globally satisfying assignment. Probabilistic models replace fixed states by stationary distributions of stochastic matrices or Markov kernels.

Branching histories can be generalized to rooted trees with branch identifiers, least common ancestors, and controlled merges. Temporal modal operators could express safety properties and characterize which are preserved by extension.

No result above asserts self-consistency of closed timelike curves in a G\"odel universe. Such a curve is geometric, while consistency depends on a law governing matter or information. A faithful treatment must first define a Lorentzian spacetime, timelike curves, the G\"odel metric, and an evolution relation. Consistency can then be posed relative to that dynamics rather than inferred from geometric closure alone.

\section{Conclusion}
A deterministic causal loop is a periodic orbit, but periodicity is not self-consistency. Under the pointwise Novikov condition, a positive closed orbit is consistent exactly when it contains a fixed point. One fixed event absorbs the future, and closure forces it to equal the start. Idempotent laws make this collapse automatic.

Boolean negation has no fixed point, no odd closed orbit, and closed positive even orbits that remain inconsistent. The grandfather contradiction is therefore the absence of a solution to a fixed-point equation, not a failure of recurrence. In the branching model, appending an intervention preserves the source and creates a strict descendant. Strict descent is transitive and irreflexive, and distinct interventions form incomparable siblings. Closure, consistency, equilibrium, and branching are thereby separated into precise mathematical notions.

\end{document}

